\ A spawned task clones the parent's live transient frames, so a frame-owned binding gains a
\ second holder whose teardown drops a reference. Without a matching claim at the clone, the
\ child's reap released a reference it never took and the parent's frame released it again,
\ which read freed bytes and then tripped the refcount assertion.
\
\ The spawned bodies deliberately do not mention the bindings: having a live transient frame that
\ holds one at spawn time is the whole trigger.

use "bytes" ;

[
  "hello, " "world" #append
  s: swap ;
  64 bytes-alloc b: swap ;
  [ "in-task" print-line ] task-scope
  s print-line
  b #len >string print-line
] call

gauge: virtual{ fixnum } ;

[
  42 >gauge g: swap ;
  [ [ "in-spawn" print-line ] spawn drop ] task-scope
  g unmake-gauge >string print-line
] call
